[{:db/id "q-115716/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9" :answer/id #uuid "92c94912-a0b4-54b4-b3a3-47eec8638466" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-115716/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" :answer/id #uuid "4784a473-5aa5-5c3d-b30c-6c731088b658" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-115716/selection/431472f1dab61ef54de031c09a96fb6a21dc69c8bc09f12797d1d686e8c9e8e6" :answer/id #uuid "bf563491-ad5a-5ac9-8788-6b9efece0562" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-115716/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" :answer/id #uuid "53c69429-a62d-5993-8ffa-eb0da87a19bc" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-115716/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" :answer/id #uuid "dde4f0ad-0037-5b3c-b516-43f3115b2b57" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-115716/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "03e974ba-dc66-5951-b168-9d91c0bd6458" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-115716/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" :answer-field/choices ["q-115716/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9" "q-115716/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" "q-115716/selection/431472f1dab61ef54de031c09a96fb6a21dc69c8bc09f12797d1d686e8c9e8e6" "q-115716/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" "q-115716/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-115716" :question/id #uuid "1fbb10a9-b2e0-5fda-819f-dfb9b75aea5b" :question/math-academy-id "q-115716" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044310/assets/a0b39135b78cefe8471c7d120850dde9b53786342679b5ed7f25af9110350607.png)\n\nWhich of the following equations could have generated the slope field shown above?\n\n- ${y}^{′}=-x(x-3)$\n- ${y}^{′}=9-{x}^{2}$\n- ${y}^{′}=x(x-2)(x-3)$" :question/worked-solution "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044310/assets/a43217f2af81c1bbacbebd8800b7ebcd4076a32d6ff61782e1fdab4cd182d5ee.png)\n\n \n\nNotice that the slopes of the slope field are horizontal along the lines $x=0$ and $x=3,$ as shown above. Therefore, the right-hand side of the differential equation should evaluate to zero on both of these lines.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each equation:\n\n- The equation ${y}^{′}=-x(x-3)$ gives the required behavior. The right-hand side evaluates to zero at $x=0$ and $x=3.$\n\n- The equation ${y}^{′}=9-{x}^{2}$ does *not* give the required behavior. The right-hand side does not evaluate to zero at both $x=0$ and $x=3.$\n\n- The equation ${y}^{′}=x(x-2)(x-3)$ does *not* give the required behavior. The right-hand side evaluates to zero at $x=0$ and $x=3,$ but it also evaluates to zero when $x=2,$ which is not a behavior exhibited by our slope field.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-115716/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ede6800b-ecfd-5ef8-a386-4aec94ddd89c"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-115716"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-120025/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" :answer/id #uuid "7ce63104-3a46-595c-9977-f8a9f853ca6b" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-120025/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" :answer/id #uuid "fc8b6ce1-975a-581a-b235-d7113b966cc0" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-120025/selection/431472f1dab61ef54de031c09a96fb6a21dc69c8bc09f12797d1d686e8c9e8e6" :answer/id #uuid "c8cd421e-f528-53bd-a774-e26e26836eef" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-120025/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" :answer/id #uuid "2551f46a-36d1-5d90-874e-758698581d60" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-120025/selection/1f1f2fe9df6485d5cdc7916416b2f79699d874348406e639d5daec02cb2e57e6" :answer/id #uuid "31a15e01-646c-5afe-97d5-879308474de7" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-120025/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "50576a3a-1a37-5b7b-b0a3-87ea7930c347" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-120025/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" :answer-field/choices ["q-120025/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" "q-120025/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" "q-120025/selection/431472f1dab61ef54de031c09a96fb6a21dc69c8bc09f12797d1d686e8c9e8e6" "q-120025/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" "q-120025/selection/1f1f2fe9df6485d5cdc7916416b2f79699d874348406e639d5daec02cb2e57e6"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-120025" :question/id #uuid "de6d895f-4494-5fa4-acc4-ab90880d6dfc" :question/math-academy-id "q-120025" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044310/assets/17cd72acbe8dd412d37c51ff992278d9664f6c5b1141a8604278fda76e57700f.png)\n\nWhich of the following equations could have generated the slope field shown above?\n\n- ${y}^{′}=x-2$\n- ${y}^{′}={x}^{2}(x-2)$\n- ${y}^{′}=x(x+1)(x-2)$" :question/worked-solution "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044310/assets/5d6165488c8e41c16cf31b1b000a924344399c2b94a8fe5e7138c55adf97602b.png)\n\nNotice that the slopes of the slope field are horizontal along the lines $x=0$ and $x=2,$ as shown above. Therefore, the right-hand side of the differential equation should evaluate to zero on both of these lines.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each equation:\n\n- The equation ${y}^{′}=x-2$ does *not* give the required behavior. The right-hand side does not evaluate to zero at both $x=0$ and $x=2.$\n\n- The equation ${y}^{′}={x}^{2}(x-2)$ gives the required behavior. The right-hand side evaluates to zero at $x=0$ and $x=2.$\n\n- The equation ${y}^{′}=x(x+1)(x-2)$ does *not* give the required behavior. The right-hand side evaluates to zero at $x=0$ and $x=2,$ but it also evaluates to zero when $x=-1,$ which is not a behavior exhibited by our slope field.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-120025/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ede6800b-ecfd-5ef8-a386-4aec94ddd89c"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-120025"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-115714/selection/f80535409f78d0e1b5cd0ceb7e72783093e3cde8adf28a5032c83c6dc6b5e490" :answer/id #uuid "abf7a6a5-3608-5733-b279-9869e2abfd22" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-115714/selection/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b" :answer/id #uuid "e483557c-fe05-5bd8-a201-73cc89ca66b3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-115714/selection/a0e0e68f25b8b46aafef3b74aff7beff7fddc6de5bf36921680b04c7b0e6ffaa" :answer/id #uuid "f9d2a1c3-0735-5a60-a422-dbc789e3be15" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "6" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-115714/selection/f853eeffac47c628af4a2b7e7b0ecd024652f548b0b0007bc18dba21ad82ad7c" :answer/id #uuid "c62b0a5b-dbed-52dd-a9be-cbc2b5b7ad77" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-6" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-115714/selection/be9eb51cc2f690a7d5b4aa400428edabf5a5d631a176c92b7f40bdd47725ed03" :answer/id #uuid "e16b6855-4ef0-57c6-a691-f967a42960e0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-115714/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "eb84f7c2-004f-5820-aa31-87d63b748491" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-115714/selection/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b" :answer-field/choices ["q-115714/selection/f80535409f78d0e1b5cd0ceb7e72783093e3cde8adf28a5032c83c6dc6b5e490" "q-115714/selection/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b" "q-115714/selection/a0e0e68f25b8b46aafef3b74aff7beff7fddc6de5bf36921680b04c7b0e6ffaa" "q-115714/selection/f853eeffac47c628af4a2b7e7b0ecd024652f548b0b0007bc18dba21ad82ad7c" "q-115714/selection/be9eb51cc2f690a7d5b4aa400428edabf5a5d631a176c92b7f40bdd47725ed03"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-115714" :question/id #uuid "614f1822-a941-5fcf-b01e-99c6019483df" :question/math-academy-id "q-115714" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044310/assets/36493e520e5f4dbc91482ab578ec4499eb00aee948171a6ea43d9d40c0e62938.png)\n\nThe differential equation that generates the slope field shown above is given by ${y}^{′}=-(x-a)(x-b).$ Find the value of $a+b.$" :question/worked-solution "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044310/assets/1da6182ac4999453b1c7e5f4c7db4d0a7e42f285463e8fe35aac5b8df46eb400.png)\n\n \n\nNotice that the slopes of the slope field are horizontal along the lines $x=-2$ and $x=4,$ as shown above. Therefore, the right-hand side of the differential equation should evaluate to zero on both of these lines.\n\nSo, our differential equation must be\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-(x+2)(x-4).\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $a=-2,b=4$ and $a+b=2.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-115714/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ede6800b-ecfd-5ef8-a386-4aec94ddd89c"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-115714"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-115706/selection/b13ac151352392d38026eed875b911708c50c896d6c09463410ac041030163ff" :answer/id #uuid "c4b4ee96-db63-5058-9321-6f3ea9a1f80a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-115706/selection/62a6fd1cf465955cead36d661dcbed2f93206049c15d21d7eebcabb802d1aa54" :answer/id #uuid "7aa57027-823c-5372-ab48-db66485a5653" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-115706/selection/be9eb51cc2f690a7d5b4aa400428edabf5a5d631a176c92b7f40bdd47725ed03" :answer/id #uuid "c396e101-2455-55f5-806e-0a94cb9ad800" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-115706/selection/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c" :answer/id #uuid "f6e45191-06df-5032-a444-b262dcdde220" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-115706/selection/e9bc05c525076ace5dedc0dc52c4f8497f441cd3bf97b666736f75acd9994b26" :answer/id #uuid "aafda417-fc40-5531-8a0f-fcaaa2d147fe" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-115706/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "da6d3baa-2837-5d69-8c1a-a590f7d12d66" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-115706/selection/e9bc05c525076ace5dedc0dc52c4f8497f441cd3bf97b666736f75acd9994b26" :answer-field/choices ["q-115706/selection/b13ac151352392d38026eed875b911708c50c896d6c09463410ac041030163ff" "q-115706/selection/62a6fd1cf465955cead36d661dcbed2f93206049c15d21d7eebcabb802d1aa54" "q-115706/selection/be9eb51cc2f690a7d5b4aa400428edabf5a5d631a176c92b7f40bdd47725ed03" "q-115706/selection/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c" "q-115706/selection/e9bc05c525076ace5dedc0dc52c4f8497f441cd3bf97b666736f75acd9994b26"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-115706" :question/id #uuid "69d6e7a9-706b-5fd1-9cdb-c1c105740396" :question/math-academy-id "q-115706" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044310/assets/883a60e9ac0d526d76243a44cff129f04ca148c1c2f0fb6a4b34db630b2193cd.png)\n\nThe differential equation that generates the slope field shown above is given by ${y}^{′}=(x-a)(x-b).$ Find the value of $a+b.$" :question/worked-solution "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044310/assets/012fe788fa1ac778b361fe62c3f70344546c6c5008fa6dcce536cc721d4ba5e9.png)\n\n \n\nNotice that the slopes of the slope field are horizontal along the lines $x=-2$ and $x=1,$ as shown above. Therefore, the right-hand side of the differential equation should evaluate to zero on both of these lines.\n\nSo, our differential equation must be\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=(x+2)(x-1).\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $a=-2,b=1$ and $a+b=-1.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-115706/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ede6800b-ecfd-5ef8-a386-4aec94ddd89c"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-115706"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-115719/selection/431472f1dab61ef54de031c09a96fb6a21dc69c8bc09f12797d1d686e8c9e8e6" :answer/id #uuid "9857310d-32ac-5e22-9c93-b0a51995bdc8" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-115719/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" :answer/id #uuid "528a6458-f976-505a-9266-f4a34e1a8df0" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-115719/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9" :answer/id #uuid "dd1ef709-3fbc-546d-9ce7-8ced2627c198" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-115719/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" :answer/id #uuid "52345efd-1752-59f3-95a3-62146364ff65" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-115719/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" :answer/id #uuid "6e860563-08e1-59dc-8636-5664bc60dcb1" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-115719/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "a0541524-54d2-5ea2-83d0-1a3f50440fae" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-115719/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" :answer-field/choices ["q-115719/selection/431472f1dab61ef54de031c09a96fb6a21dc69c8bc09f12797d1d686e8c9e8e6" "q-115719/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" "q-115719/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9" "q-115719/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" "q-115719/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-115719" :question/id #uuid "9e78b269-9005-5d97-be46-7b9aca10039f" :question/math-academy-id "q-115719" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044310/assets/170ddea2148f369d4feedd14540f234d7946bb6670d1ca6eb4c4ebbbb086a760.png)\n\nWhich of the following equations could have generated the slope field shown above?\n\n- ${y}^{′}=(x+3)(x+7)$\n- ${y}^{′}=x(x+3)$\n- ${y}^{′}=(x+3)(x+5)$" :question/worked-solution "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044310/assets/cf77e09457cd6b53da801a6eb876a79712935eba623c851a5b6dafa58887a076.png)\n\n \n\nNotice that the slopes of the slope field are horizontal along the lines $x=-3$ and $x=-7,$ as shown above. Therefore, the right-hand side of the differential equation should evaluate to zero on both of these lines.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each equation:\n\n- The equation ${y}^{′}=(x+3)(x+7)$ gives the required behavior. The right-hand side evaluates to zero at $x=-7$ and $x=-3.$\n\n- The equation ${y}^{′}=x(x+3)$ does *not* give the required behavior. The right-hand side does not evaluate to zero at $x=-7,$ and also evaluates to zero when $x=0,$ which is not a behavior exhibited by our slope field.\n\n- The equation ${y}^{′}=(x+3)(x+5)$ does *not* give the required behavior. The right-hand side evaluates to zero at $x=-3$ but it also evaluates to zero when $x=-5,$ which is not a behavior exhibited by our slope field.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-115719/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ede6800b-ecfd-5ef8-a386-4aec94ddd89c"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-115719"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-96095"] :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044310/assets/252d52b07c6c5e7a43c7816e3eb769e3f5abb561b84ef144ba0eeff022557260.png)\n\nThe diagram shown above is the slope field for which of the following differential equations?" :question/worked-solution "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044310/assets/dfd308fd43e27e7adaab6d40411cf33e53f0186faf5719c1ccbe9d934f552cca.png)\n\n \n\nNotice the following properties of the given slope field:\n\n- First, the slopes of the slope field are horizontal along the lines $x=-2$ and $x=1.$ Therefore, the right-hand side of the differential equation should equal zero on both of these lines.\n\nOf the given equations, the only equations that satisfy this condition are the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =(x-1)(x+2)^{2} \\\\ {y}^{′} & =(x-1)(x+2) \\\\ {y}^{′} & =x(x-1)(x+2)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can dismiss the equation ${y}^{′}=x(x-1)(x+2).$ Although the right-hand side evaluates to zero at $x=-2$ and $x=1,$ it also evaluates to zero when $x=0,$ which is behavior not exhibited by our slope field.\n\n- Second, the lines $x=-2$ and $x=1$ divide the plane into $3$ regions. The sign of the slopes in each of the $3$ regions are easily deduced by considering the behavior of the solution curves in these regions. This information is summarized in the following table:\n\n| Region | $x<-2$ | $-2<x<1$ | $x>1$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Slope | $↘$ | $↘$ | $↗$ |\n| Sign | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n\nOf the two remaining options, the only one that gives this behavior is\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=(x-1)(x+2)^{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is ${y}^{′}=(x-1)(x+2)^{2}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "q-115675/selection/182d0078ffe5e9e2e7f7f5fba37a5b109c4dd354d9c9ef17258a3d4e5c4cdf8f" :answer/id #uuid "f8a011c8-3d57-5f03-a22a-dc85c39067d3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{y}^{′}=(1-x)(x-4)^{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-115675/selection/394c6cc1fcb3dc1fe048da3284f8ec7989f109d43ea8e23e74de0c195b4680e6" :answer/id #uuid "2b332937-f022-5518-8f46-86946ab3ed07" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{y}^{′}=2(4-x)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-115675/selection/ac6cc92cc0a409a25d45ce8b21c81a844123bd698736644555dc0f133f70a412" :answer/id #uuid "1d3c0da9-361a-5059-94dc-9a1b2b6bf862" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{y}^{′}=\\frac{1}{3}(x-1)^{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-115675/selection/38af65361f06f450edb2e02030d1f2627a1affa597a977917a3c5a265a54c05a" :answer/id #uuid "f298011f-e2cb-56eb-bcfb-94db3a01dd93" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{y}^{′}=(x-4)^{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-115675/selection/4b35189d4365bed26019215eda523e7e62dd2d784a02a4780ae8c299b3edc6fb" :answer/id #uuid "3d80a7a3-bc56-59b6-9d07-722e2122d028" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{y}^{′}=\\frac{1}{2}(1-x)(x-4)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-115675/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "54250b60-3dcc-5209-b3f8-a4bdd24497bb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-115675/selection/4b35189d4365bed26019215eda523e7e62dd2d784a02a4780ae8c299b3edc6fb" :answer-field/choices ["q-115675/selection/182d0078ffe5e9e2e7f7f5fba37a5b109c4dd354d9c9ef17258a3d4e5c4cdf8f" "q-115675/selection/394c6cc1fcb3dc1fe048da3284f8ec7989f109d43ea8e23e74de0c195b4680e6" "q-115675/selection/ac6cc92cc0a409a25d45ce8b21c81a844123bd698736644555dc0f133f70a412" "q-115675/selection/38af65361f06f450edb2e02030d1f2627a1affa597a977917a3c5a265a54c05a" "q-115675/selection/4b35189d4365bed26019215eda523e7e62dd2d784a02a4780ae8c299b3edc6fb"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-115675" :question/id #uuid "3df61d29-a012-5fca-b47e-3b1880e87b99" :question/math-academy-id "q-115675" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044310/assets/0d1dba03b8b14af89dbf06bf84f02c1a15f4f47f230900d4c46de87f14baeaf9.png)\n\nThe diagram shown above is the slope field for which of the following differential equations?" :question/worked-solution "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044310/assets/afb50fc9034d42ae57b9861dec4a2f5d0acf4f0c7122fc1ce845f5e3d60988fe.png)\n\n \n\nNotice the following properties of the given slope field:\n\n- First, the slopes of the slope field are horizontal along the lines $x=1$ and $x=4.$ Therefore, the right-hand side of the differential equation should equal zero on both of these lines.\n\nOf the given equations, the only equations that satisfy this condition are the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =\\frac{1}{2}(1-x)(x-4) \\\\ {y}^{′} & =(1-x)(x-4)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Second, the lines $x=1$ and $x=4$ divide the plane into $3$ regions. The sign of the slopes in each of the $3$ regions are easily deduced by considering the behavior of the solution curves in these regions. This information is summarized in the following table:\n\n| Region | $x<1$ | $1<x<4$ | $x>4$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Slope | $↘$ | $↗$ | $↘$ |\n| Sign | $-$ | $+$ | $-$ |\n\nOf the three remaining options, the only one that gives this behavior is\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\frac{1}{2}(1-x)(x-4).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is ${y}^{′}=\\frac{1}{2}(1-x)(x-4).$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-115675/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5277f56d-3f2d-505c-bf73-48207a2310a3"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-115675"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-115677/selection/c7d0a2c3d5aea5f4216568ba0e76e2367b75aba2adff85055b22542026530c22" :answer/id #uuid "7e280158-9163-55d6-b409-4dd79b177afc" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{y}^{′}=(x+1)^{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-115677/selection/a1eefdfb655ec94e94e5ec3bdfb481436d25cf35f02af465f247fbe0e3794fc1" :answer/id #uuid "0eefe24d-6d4c-5c03-b6bf-406e33bdb895" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{y}^{′}=x(x+1)(x+3)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-115677/selection/75102dcf8c7b41d54d73f1f2b3dc8733083efc03c05254bab942bb6d3a4b0437" :answer/id #uuid "d31fa0e4-86ef-56e0-9434-4ca43785c92e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{y}^{′}=x(x+1)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-115677/selection/b0e8c693794ba0e29c66d07eafe62f5af78ae035d5a79555accf130c16845dfa" :answer/id #uuid "82a67a14-25c2-58f6-bfab-f2d3c8236a37" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y=x(x+3)^{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-115677/selection/410612569f639b9a9aedc8d13198ffc677615ac141e9435a0bd9f5beccada572" :answer/id #uuid "d1331c82-fefa-5195-a7e2-5beb4fa7b605" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{y}^{′}=x(x+3)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-115677/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "31ded76c-c435-5454-a016-738124f90b45" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-115677/selection/410612569f639b9a9aedc8d13198ffc677615ac141e9435a0bd9f5beccada572" :answer-field/choices ["q-115677/selection/c7d0a2c3d5aea5f4216568ba0e76e2367b75aba2adff85055b22542026530c22" "q-115677/selection/a1eefdfb655ec94e94e5ec3bdfb481436d25cf35f02af465f247fbe0e3794fc1" "q-115677/selection/75102dcf8c7b41d54d73f1f2b3dc8733083efc03c05254bab942bb6d3a4b0437" "q-115677/selection/b0e8c693794ba0e29c66d07eafe62f5af78ae035d5a79555accf130c16845dfa" "q-115677/selection/410612569f639b9a9aedc8d13198ffc677615ac141e9435a0bd9f5beccada572"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-115677" :question/id #uuid "ba1cbc26-b6c5-5882-ab7e-b2a130fa1c84" :question/math-academy-id "q-115677" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044310/assets/b16e7007b3a266291fa22c1760e55e5b5e0a3e9a2d4ea97303f379a1f372cd32.png)\n\nThe diagram shown above is the slope field for which of the following differential equations?" :question/worked-solution "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044310/assets/d84c6743bd4cc1ab3306951f507f4691ea3c3f2075ead5f04328f64254d5a786.png)\n\n \n\nNotice the following properties of the given slope field:\n\n- First, the slopes of the slope field are horizontal along the lines $x=-3$ and $x=0.$ Therefore, the right-hand side of the differential equation should equal zero on both of these lines.\n\nOf the given equations, the only equations that satisfy this condition are the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =x(x+3) \\\\ {y}^{′} & =x(x+1)(x+3) \\\\ {y}^{′} & =x(x+3)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can dismiss the equation ${y}^{′}=x(x+1)(x+3).$ Although the right-hand side evaluates to zero at $x=-3$ and $x=0,$ it also evaluates to zero when $x=-1,$ which is behavior not exhibited by our slope field.\n\n- Second, the lines $x=-3$ and $x=0$ divide the plane into $3$ regions. The sign of the slopes in each of the $3$ regions are easily deduced by considering the behavior of the solution curves in these regions. This information is summarized in the following table:\n\n| Region | $x<-3$ | $-3<x<0$ | $x>0$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Slope | $↗$ | $↘$ | $↗$ |\n| Sign | $+$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n\nOf the two remaining options, the only one that gives this behavior is\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=x(x+3).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is ${y}^{′}=x(x+3).$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-115677/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5277f56d-3f2d-505c-bf73-48207a2310a3"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-115677"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-115672/selection/cbf77fd8ee650c7816bb099cdcf3b1e395297d2c6dbd78ea9b229576da125742" :answer/id #uuid "2d42075f-5a58-58e3-9473-65b74c1a6a04" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{y}^{′}=x(x-2)^{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-115672/selection/d9df92efad3a9b0d4a93d3a84200c09fbff82446208aeedb359ce73323997fde" :answer/id #uuid "ee002fde-161b-5b29-96f6-78b758089c8e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{y}^{′}=(x-2)^{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-115672/selection/59fc194c61c0af37be2c6188db6994d328272e7ebae87908e3bd7eba61669dcf" :answer/id #uuid "472fd94e-0014-55ac-9a8b-c617eaa96bda" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{y}^{′}={x}^{2}(x+2)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-115672/selection/0315ee9cfcca582ef3cf273fcccc628b9daa1e9da2e17f98e2991a665c1accfb" :answer/id #uuid "e44065c8-00f8-5235-a6b5-09994d41088f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{y}^{′}=-x(x+2)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-115672/selection/950e3b7909543f44752bf2c8fc488b185f0a90c084fca1ebf18ed45205a51630" :answer/id #uuid "8d1a4c48-ade2-5642-a8ed-186b875534e5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{y}^{′}={x}^{2}(2-x)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-115672/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "05e0262f-9154-5c98-8c69-c1a7929da18d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-115672/selection/950e3b7909543f44752bf2c8fc488b185f0a90c084fca1ebf18ed45205a51630" :answer-field/choices ["q-115672/selection/cbf77fd8ee650c7816bb099cdcf3b1e395297d2c6dbd78ea9b229576da125742" "q-115672/selection/d9df92efad3a9b0d4a93d3a84200c09fbff82446208aeedb359ce73323997fde" "q-115672/selection/59fc194c61c0af37be2c6188db6994d328272e7ebae87908e3bd7eba61669dcf" "q-115672/selection/0315ee9cfcca582ef3cf273fcccc628b9daa1e9da2e17f98e2991a665c1accfb" "q-115672/selection/950e3b7909543f44752bf2c8fc488b185f0a90c084fca1ebf18ed45205a51630"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-115672" :question/id #uuid "433ce550-1d93-55af-8282-9fdcc1fd2d1e" :question/math-academy-id "q-115672" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044310/assets/a9c84c00dfdd0f84e8bf81dc4be8808794685a884f0e6bebd908da461e3de9a4.png)\n\nThe diagram shown above is the slope field for which of the following differential equations?" :question/worked-solution "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044310/assets/d7f1fccf2bb73f160faa04c058db6892f78f105e374516c97558f81121fc49f8.png)\n\n \n\nNotice the following properties of the given slope field:\n\n- First, the slopes of the slope field are horizontal along the lines $x=0$ and $x=2.$ Therefore, the right-hand side of the differential equation should equal zero on both of these lines.\n\nOf the given equations, the only equations that satisfy this condition are the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & ={x}^{2}(2-x) \\\\ {y}^{′} & =x(x-2)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Second, the lines $x=0$ and $x=2$ divide the plane into $3$ regions. The sign of the slopes in each of the $3$ regions are easily deduced by considering the behavior of the solution curves in these regions. This information is summarized in the following table:\n\n| Region | $x<0$ | $0<x<2$ | $x>2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Slope | $↗$ | $↗$ | $↘$ |\n| Sign | $+$ | $+$ | $-$ |\n\nOf the three remaining options, the only one that gives this behavior is\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}={x}^{2}(2-x).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is ${y}^{′}={x}^{2}(2-x).$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-115672/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5277f56d-3f2d-505c-bf73-48207a2310a3"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-115672"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-96094"] :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044310/assets/76c15fa584f5a216d23ef10b10fbae14c09b84714e49bd8db0cc7954cab8c0f3.png)\n\nWhich of the following equations could have generated the slope field shown above?\n\n- ${y}^{′}=x-2$\n- ${y}^{′}=x(x-2)^{2}$\n- ${y}^{′}=x(x-2)$" :question/worked-solution "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044310/assets/111e679939475cabbfe9a40779de3fa1bf4f2de732f943a3b0396a3ee9bf3519.png)\n\n \n\nNotice the following properties of the given slope field:\n\n- First, the slopes of the slope field are horizontal along the lines $x=0$ and $x=2.$ Therefore, the right-hand side of the differential equation should evaluate to zero on both of these lines.\n\n- Second, the lines $x=0$ and $x=2$ divide the plane into $3$ regions. The sign of the slopes in each of the $3$ regions are easily deduced by considering the behavior of the solution curves in these regions. This information is summarized in the following table:\n\n| Region | $x<0$ | $0<x<2$ | $x>2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Slope | $↗$ | $↘$ | $↗$ |\n| Sign | $+$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each equation.\n\n- The equation ${y}^{′}=x-2$ does *not* give the required behavior. The right-hand side evaluates to zero at $x=2$ only.\n\n- The equation ${y}^{′}=x(x-2)^{2}$ does *not* give the required behavior. The right-hand side evaluates to zero at $x=0$ and $x=2,$ but the sign of the slopes in the required regions (summarized below) do not correspond to the given slope field.\n\n| Region | $x<0$ | $0<x<2$ | $x>2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Slope | $↘$ | $↗$ | $↗$ |\n| Sign | $-$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n\n- The equation ${y}^{′}=x(x-2)$ gives the required behavior.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "q-115813/selection/1f1f2fe9df6485d5cdc7916416b2f79699d874348406e639d5daec02cb2e57e6" :answer/id #uuid "1b7cedd6-9f5d-5fd0-9ae1-9d68dd04e8a4" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-115813/selection/c5361bc7e0ee1fc99d41021ab98e1ca699e408307178f1c31af6bc0071e78ea0" :answer/id #uuid "0ac9c0a5-e8af-54ae-a66f-2394f60aebfd" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "None of those listed" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-115813/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" :answer/id #uuid "c55827d4-022b-5bd7-b813-e5419984bc4c" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-115813/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" :answer/id #uuid "38425ced-3908-5671-ab4c-2164d3556e87" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-115813/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" :answer/id #uuid "ef2f1d42-3783-5fea-80aa-5283f5cdafd0" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-115813/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "d7cef83a-903c-5eeb-a517-b362405e9050" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-115813/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" :answer-field/choices ["q-115813/selection/1f1f2fe9df6485d5cdc7916416b2f79699d874348406e639d5daec02cb2e57e6" "q-115813/selection/c5361bc7e0ee1fc99d41021ab98e1ca699e408307178f1c31af6bc0071e78ea0" "q-115813/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" "q-115813/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" "q-115813/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-115813" :question/id #uuid "1cdd2071-3572-5d87-bf79-9e3a992ff987" :question/math-academy-id "q-115813" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044310/assets/5a2a2b4b141f4f0c250e4865b174b9eb32b1ed4ab94cc6ebd074131673d5712c.png)\n\nWhich of the following equations could have generated the slope field shown above?\n\n- ${y}^{′}=-2{x}^{2}-4x-2$\n- ${y}^{′}={x}^{2}+2x+1$\n- ${y}^{′}={x}^{2}-1$" :question/worked-solution "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044310/assets/69465ce0573d7b6deac631663766a5dd1c90afa29eeb0c5286cb5ef1f7ee1153.png)\n\n \n\nNotice the following properties of the given slope field:\n\n- First, the slope field is zero along the line $x=-1.$ Therefore, the right-hand side of the differential equation should evaluate to zero on this line.\n\n- Second, the line $x=-1$ divides the plane into $2$ regions. The sign of the slopes in each of the $2$ regions are easily deduced by considering the behavior of the solution curves in these regions. This information is summarized in the following table:\n\n| Region | $x<-1$ | $x>-1$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Slope | $↗$ | $↗$ |\n| Sign | $+$ | $+$ |\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each equation.\n\n- The equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-2{x}^{2}-4x-2=-2(x+1)^{2}\n$$\n\ndoes *not* give the required behavior. The right-hand side evaluates to zero at $x=-1,$ but the sign of the slopes in the required regions (summarized below) do not correspond to the given slope field.\n\n| Region | $x<-1$ | $x>-1$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Slope | $↘$ | $↘$ |\n| Sign | $-$ | $-$ |\n\n- The equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}={x}^{2}+2x+1=(x+1)^{2}\n$$\n\ngives the required behavior.\n\n- The equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}={x}^{2}-1=(x+1)(x-1)\n$$\n\ndoes *not* give the required behavior. The right-hand side evaluates to zero at $x=-1$ and $x=1.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-115813/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "762c5225-00bd-5d2f-9859-394f886482cb"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-115813"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-120072/selection/148decc28bf5aa85dd84c685b7338015914dd65b26bb11207400996db1018466" :answer/id #uuid "5bb14e93-6a33-55bd-a50b-17a08910e90d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{y}^{′}={x}^{2}+x" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-120072/selection/d4377451e5b2ebc8012bcf57feb3566a9ae1dcfa4cefd51be06c884e8612215d" :answer/id #uuid "88b26a1c-9ca1-564d-92f8-0b73c99cfab4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{y}^{′}={x}^{3}+1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-120072/selection/f9da26c87d647e1e91bd7ef9f72aafdbdf6669ef9e279c16afeae6f5ab2e94cf" :answer/id #uuid "422f484f-ee93-55e8-9ec1-484f460bcadf" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{y}^{′}={x}^{3}+{x}^{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-120072/selection/cfe55ef7c38ffd75a466826b0480b8a4251068ba0a885ca290dc0281a5f63087" :answer/id #uuid "9648aff0-ddf2-518f-abed-bafe568d4c62" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{y}^{′}={x}^{3}-{x}^{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-120072/selection/1c3ad3f235d258e158034348fea12c4f88fcf99ae377d781be13901bf5fdb0c8" :answer/id #uuid "faf5dbc3-e436-5002-9d0f-a514258f2604" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{y}^{′}=1-{x}^{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-120072/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "be10418b-4747-5ff1-95e8-cd17aa91b105" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-120072/selection/f9da26c87d647e1e91bd7ef9f72aafdbdf6669ef9e279c16afeae6f5ab2e94cf" :answer-field/choices ["q-120072/selection/148decc28bf5aa85dd84c685b7338015914dd65b26bb11207400996db1018466" "q-120072/selection/d4377451e5b2ebc8012bcf57feb3566a9ae1dcfa4cefd51be06c884e8612215d" "q-120072/selection/f9da26c87d647e1e91bd7ef9f72aafdbdf6669ef9e279c16afeae6f5ab2e94cf" "q-120072/selection/cfe55ef7c38ffd75a466826b0480b8a4251068ba0a885ca290dc0281a5f63087" "q-120072/selection/1c3ad3f235d258e158034348fea12c4f88fcf99ae377d781be13901bf5fdb0c8"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-120072" :question/id #uuid "e93b7591-d0af-5d87-a1e4-0c5a1ef93f64" :question/math-academy-id "q-120072" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044310/assets/6e57e25ea43f23e9d157b3fa1c621749d3d1a88bab389af66449c915d9e03606.png)\n\nThe diagram shown above is the slope field for which of the following differential equations?" :question/worked-solution "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044310/assets/523f18dc7b081ecf6dcfa1f09c6fe715012fffba3e4e07d1dfd44c3d53cf702c.png)\n\nNotice the following properties of the given slope field:\n\n- First, the slope field is zero along the lines $x=-1$ and $x=0.$ Therefore, the right-hand side of the differential equation should evaluate to zero on both of these lines.\n\nOf the given equations, the only two that satisfy this condition are\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}={x}^{3}+{x}^{2}={x}^{2}(x+1),\n$$\n\n \nand\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}={x}^{2}+x=x(x+1).\n$$\n\n- Second, the lines $x=-1$ and $x=0$ divide the plane into $3$ regions. The sign of the slopes in each of the $3$ regions are easily deduced by considering the behavior of the solution curves in these regions. This information is summarized in the following table:\n\n| Region | $x<-1$ | $-1<x<0$ | $x>0$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Slope | $↘$ | $↗$ | $↗$ |\n| Sign | $-$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n\nOf the two remaining options, the only one that gives this behavior is\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}={x}^{3}+{x}^{2}={x}^{2}(x+1).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is ${y}^{′}={x}^{3}+{x}^{2}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-120072/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "762c5225-00bd-5d2f-9859-394f886482cb"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-120072"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-120070/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" :answer/id #uuid "634c4288-a0dd-56c1-ba08-07d3310ba18b" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-120070/selection/1f1f2fe9df6485d5cdc7916416b2f79699d874348406e639d5daec02cb2e57e6" :answer/id #uuid "b9eff459-3c71-5895-8fae-3d6beecb694a" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-120070/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" :answer/id #uuid "da55894b-e0db-5544-a761-bf9776533701" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-120070/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" :answer/id #uuid "e6d3acca-9253-5619-947f-f12026672040" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-120070/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9" :answer/id #uuid "70be9107-e562-5579-9575-baaa6d128076" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-120070/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "59254fd9-0cc0-54a3-871b-0afc57beb24c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-120070/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" :answer-field/choices ["q-120070/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" "q-120070/selection/1f1f2fe9df6485d5cdc7916416b2f79699d874348406e639d5daec02cb2e57e6" "q-120070/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" "q-120070/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" "q-120070/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-120070" :question/id #uuid "2ce3677b-f81c-5f71-ba2d-0905f9bffa60" :question/math-academy-id "q-120070" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044310/assets/51273365c4a282dc3dedd9d7cd5ac7d805e1526aaeee1915e8bd95d8223c26d3.png)\n\nWhich of the following equations could have generated the slope field shown above?\n\n- ${y}^{′}={x}^{2}+3x$\n- ${y}^{′}={x}^{2}+x$\n- ${y}^{′}={x}^{3}+3{x}^{2}$" :question/worked-solution "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044310/assets/a14651ffc883a8c24ad17bdd031658c3d4ae21db2cfded213f2ba911fdb9a765.png)\n\nNotice the following properties of the given slope field:\n\n- First, the slope field is zero along the lines $x=-3$ and $x=0.$ Therefore, the right-hand side of the differential equation should evaluate to zero on both of these lines.\n\n- Second, the lines $x=-3$ and $x=0$ divide the plane into $3$ regions. The sign of the slopes in each of the $3$ regions are easily deduced by considering the behavior of the solution curves in these regions. This information is summarized in the following table:\n\n| Region | $x<-3$ | $-3<x<0$ | $x>0$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Slope | $↗$ | $↘$ | $↗$ |\n| Sign | $+$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each equation.\n\n- The equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}={x}^{2}+3x=x(x+3)\n$$\n\ngives the required behavior.\n\n- The equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}={x}^{2}+x=x(x+1)\n$$\n\ndoes *not* give the required behavior. The right-hand side evaluates to zero at $x=-1$ and $x=0.$\n\n- The equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}={x}^{3}+3{x}^{2}={x}^{2}(x+3)\n$$\n\ndoes *not* give the required behavior. The right-hand side evaluates to zero at $x=-3$ and $x=0,$ but the sign of the slopes in the required regions (summarized below) do not correspond to the given slope field.\n\n| Region | $x<-3$ | $-3<x<0$ | $x>0$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Slope | $↘$ | $↗$ | $↗$ |\n| Sign | $-$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-120070/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "762c5225-00bd-5d2f-9859-394f886482cb"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-120070"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-115846/selection/83e667558d5ce4a923fa3f6af9812599de3cdefe8c6d98f505e34dbe4d4048b5" :answer/id #uuid "693f9278-e6fa-5e4f-8c06-1d334c50df53" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{y}^{′}=x+{x}^{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-115846/selection/3c4e301230899f702c3ad4bb2585d1b87fa6566d30aa6b1a096829d4163aa548" :answer/id #uuid "9729fed7-a20f-53c9-aa2f-d8cc3927d53b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{y}^{′}=x(x+1)^{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-115846/selection/0fc64f31c4e77cc0eb1f31a03dc44d6ede7e73aa7c4cf3c1101577c29748b9a2" :answer/id #uuid "84280866-3ae3-562d-84f2-208ae85270ba" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{y}^{′}=(x-1)^{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-115846/selection/e3e1167be00cc5c17ac0cab9dfab74e02ea103efdebd4277651210598fdcf8bd" :answer/id #uuid "7e9df385-dfbf-5f70-8bd4-0cb9d2e855ef" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{y}^{′}=2{x}^{2}-2{x}^{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-115846/selection/ff491f2044af2d820c618f1585a206779f783623305e2616cbb87dc0093a8fa7" :answer/id #uuid "3d2593d9-de29-5ff0-bbad-93c290ada27c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{y}^{′}={x}^{3}-2{x}^{2}+x" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-115846/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "2718120b-4462-54ac-bc13-1da56dd43a88" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-115846/selection/e3e1167be00cc5c17ac0cab9dfab74e02ea103efdebd4277651210598fdcf8bd" :answer-field/choices ["q-115846/selection/83e667558d5ce4a923fa3f6af9812599de3cdefe8c6d98f505e34dbe4d4048b5" "q-115846/selection/3c4e301230899f702c3ad4bb2585d1b87fa6566d30aa6b1a096829d4163aa548" "q-115846/selection/0fc64f31c4e77cc0eb1f31a03dc44d6ede7e73aa7c4cf3c1101577c29748b9a2" "q-115846/selection/e3e1167be00cc5c17ac0cab9dfab74e02ea103efdebd4277651210598fdcf8bd" "q-115846/selection/ff491f2044af2d820c618f1585a206779f783623305e2616cbb87dc0093a8fa7"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-115846" :question/id #uuid "98ce449b-be7b-52eb-a7f7-5eaea5676c55" :question/math-academy-id "q-115846" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044310/assets/b67f4648af37df7332a42a671fd8c3f7b0d7a8f626dc3ed9c4f32c1bb633f258.png)\n\nThe diagram shown above is the slope field for which of the following differential equations?" :question/worked-solution "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044310/assets/ef7114aaf0f2cc275b79fe312ac6e99bda2bf07667eaa4ccfaae3920538381b5.png)\n\n \n\nNotice the following properties of the given slope field:\n\n- First, the slope field is zero along the lines $x=0$ and $x=1.$ Therefore, the right-hand side of the differential equation should evaluate to zero on both of these lines.\n\nOf the given equations, the only two that satisfy this condition are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =2{x}^{2}-2{x}^{3}=2{x}^{2}(1-x),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & ={x}^{3}-2{x}^{2}+x=x(x-1)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Second, the lines $x=0$ and $x=1$ divide the plane into $3$ regions. The sign of the slopes in each of the $3$ regions is easily deduced by considering the behavior of the solution curves in these regions. This information is summarized in the following table:\n\n| Region | $x<0$ | $0<x<1$ | $x>1$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Slope | $↗$ | $↗$ | $↘$ |\n| Sign | $+$ | $+$ | $-$ |\n\nOf the two remaining options, the only one that gives this behavior is\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=2{x}^{2}-2{x}^{3}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is ${y}^{′}=2{x}^{2}-2{x}^{3}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-115846/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "762c5225-00bd-5d2f-9859-394f886482cb"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-115846"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-115850/selection/3e05c1b719e7489c7f9ed9e401d2c9d304f1c0d7b369977a9d16c0e74ef8ae3a" :answer/id #uuid "1120938d-6049-5580-8087-d3676b62af4c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{y}^{′}={x}^{3}-3{x}^{2}+2x" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-115850/selection/1017e2300c54b803dcb7be5b70bb8489e1478d76de705e7f76fb43c462c0067c" :answer/id #uuid "396f0be6-bfcf-524e-9cf1-0c6de878d6c8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{y}^{′}=-2(x+1)(x-3)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-115850/selection/47b7028b3e8a1c9161245cbfaa32d5cc3d40721696bbe59f12a3c8c5e194e13c" :answer/id #uuid "285ba4b8-054d-58bc-ae0f-a1af572214d9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{y}^{′}={x}^{2}-9" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-115850/selection/1caa1132287c8705c754b7f6ab319a5279522266d846c892216c7a22927a57c6" :answer/id #uuid "8603513e-628c-5ca0-9a7b-1bc5412c1f52" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{y}^{′}={x}^{2}-1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-115850/selection/dc0ebeaf86a528bae88bca2541903468d02bd70c407caa881edbec473a98f08b" :answer/id #uuid "73697fb2-bac9-50a7-9e1d-7aea9a677ccb" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{y}^{′}={x}^{2}-2x-3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-115850/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "bc9b8b92-ddf4-59db-bdef-6d67b3d66f05" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-115850/selection/dc0ebeaf86a528bae88bca2541903468d02bd70c407caa881edbec473a98f08b" :answer-field/choices ["q-115850/selection/3e05c1b719e7489c7f9ed9e401d2c9d304f1c0d7b369977a9d16c0e74ef8ae3a" "q-115850/selection/1017e2300c54b803dcb7be5b70bb8489e1478d76de705e7f76fb43c462c0067c" "q-115850/selection/47b7028b3e8a1c9161245cbfaa32d5cc3d40721696bbe59f12a3c8c5e194e13c" "q-115850/selection/1caa1132287c8705c754b7f6ab319a5279522266d846c892216c7a22927a57c6" "q-115850/selection/dc0ebeaf86a528bae88bca2541903468d02bd70c407caa881edbec473a98f08b"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-115850" :question/id #uuid "d64821a5-cde7-5ce2-b79e-de10dc003b21" :question/math-academy-id "q-115850" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044310/assets/b2af712f3b4cca278137bd358d0f889b9b2a63b3ae3d54d3c699b974f43d3a06.png)\n\nThe diagram shown above is the slope field for which of the following differential equations?" :question/worked-solution "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044310/assets/0034c86bbb1f4412cba2a7d202170b87fcd2570789a37affb88b399b085b33f6.png)\n\n \n\nNotice the following properties of the given slope field:\n\n- First, the slope field is zero along the lines $x=-1$ and $x=3.$ Therefore, the right-hand side of the differential equation should evaluate to zero on both of these lines.\n\nOf the given equations, the only two that satisfy this condition are\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}={x}^{2}-2x-3=(x+1)(x-3)\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-2(x+1)(x-3).\n$$\n\n- Second, the lines $x=-1$ and $x=3$ divide the plane into $3$ regions. The sign of the slopes in each of the $3$ regions is easily deduced by considering the behavior of the solution curves in these regions. This information is summarized in the following table:\n\n| Region | $x<-1$ | $-1<x<3$ | $x>3$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Slope | $↗$ | $↘$ | $↗$ |\n| Sign | $+$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n\nOf the two remaining options, the only one that gives this behavior is\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}={x}^{2}-2x-3.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is ${y}^{′}={x}^{2}-2x-3.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-115850/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "762c5225-00bd-5d2f-9859-394f886482cb"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-115850"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-120076/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9" :answer/id #uuid "611c5f45-2c55-5299-8899-0a975b89ec2f" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-120076/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" :answer/id #uuid "dfefb2a8-24a9-5577-bef8-df1ab86daf60" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-120076/selection/1f1f2fe9df6485d5cdc7916416b2f79699d874348406e639d5daec02cb2e57e6" :answer/id #uuid "b38e3344-d887-5b82-83d3-39edc3d44b9f" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-120076/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" :answer/id #uuid "3aa45128-67e1-5faa-b707-eaeb146f7df4" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-120076/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" :answer/id #uuid "ebce7546-6377-51ad-9544-4da707ba6944" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-120076/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "fc1afc83-bac3-5e7f-be96-8297d729796b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-120076/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" :answer-field/choices ["q-120076/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9" "q-120076/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" "q-120076/selection/1f1f2fe9df6485d5cdc7916416b2f79699d874348406e639d5daec02cb2e57e6" "q-120076/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" "q-120076/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-120076" :question/id #uuid "875466a4-b8ac-5bb9-91c1-86f9987a0d97" :question/math-academy-id "q-120076" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044310/assets/5fdb2ddd3d07537c1e3d0d22d435928f7cd11dc7a71c67f1bf45292c82a61ef7.png)\n\nWhich of the following equations could have generated the slope field shown above?\n\n- ${y}^{′}={x}^{2}(x+1)$\n- ${y}^{′}=\\frac{1}{x+1}$\n- ${y}^{′}=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{x+1}$" :question/worked-solution "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044310/assets/cf2ba44d8cc21ade2204a3784af7d09b492843339a8b98c4cde741b30dfa54db.png)\n\nNotice the following properties of the given slope field:\n\n- The solution curves have the vertical asymptote $x=-1.$\n\n- The slopes of the slope field become vertical as $x\\to \\pm \\infty .$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each equation.\n\n- The equation ${y}^{′}={x}^{2}(x+1)$ does *not* give the required behavior. The right-hand side does not approach $\\pm \\infty $ as $x\\to -1.$\n\n- The equation ${y}^{′}=\\frac{1}{x+1}$ does *not* give the required behavior. Notice that\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{x+1}\\to 0\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to \\pm \\infty .\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slopes of the slope field for this differential equation do not approach $\\infty $ as $x\\to \\pm \\infty .$\n\n- The equation ${y}^{′}=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{x+1}$ gives the required behavior. Notice that\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{x+1}=\\frac{x}{(1+\\frac{1}{x})}\\to \\pm \\infty \\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to \\pm \\infty .\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slopes of the slope field for this differential equation become vertical as $x\\to \\pm \\infty .$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-120076/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "32b9a92b-880b-50de-b8e9-648fa219b83f"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-120076"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-115751/selection/431472f1dab61ef54de031c09a96fb6a21dc69c8bc09f12797d1d686e8c9e8e6" :answer/id #uuid "68bab059-fafc-54bc-af24-3866220626ee" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-115751/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" :answer/id #uuid "2783c7c6-b2a3-5273-8e88-2968f3f84258" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-115751/selection/1f1f2fe9df6485d5cdc7916416b2f79699d874348406e639d5daec02cb2e57e6" :answer/id #uuid "0791d30d-e8ac-5c40-9c1e-b6e9b05bd55d" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-115751/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" :answer/id #uuid "4b661b36-817d-51d6-a9b1-df6e856ecd27" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-115751/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" :answer/id #uuid "e82d5972-64d3-5a30-b2a7-a205ff620ef2" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-115751/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "c203b590-c954-5726-98e3-886eb268566a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-115751/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" :answer-field/choices ["q-115751/selection/431472f1dab61ef54de031c09a96fb6a21dc69c8bc09f12797d1d686e8c9e8e6" "q-115751/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" "q-115751/selection/1f1f2fe9df6485d5cdc7916416b2f79699d874348406e639d5daec02cb2e57e6" "q-115751/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" "q-115751/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-115751" :question/id #uuid "5c45382e-150e-5edb-b139-4af7e110daac" :question/math-academy-id "q-115751" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044310/assets/2552222c6a0be1b1bf1e67a66b4906bb735c2d974d724d3e25e988d15388c696.png)\n\nWhich of the following equations could have generated the slope field shown above?\n\n- ${y}^{′}=\\frac{1}{1-{x}^{2}}$\n- ${y}^{′}=-\\frac{8}{x+1}$\n- ${y}^{′}=-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{x+1}$" :question/worked-solution "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044310/assets/eb1303132a4fe6a26c85b4733a3039a52109b4c49408e1c82038d71885d27f70.png)\n\n \n\nNotice the following properties of the given slope field:\n\n- The solution curves have the vertical asymptote $x=-1.$\n\n- The slopes of the slope field become horizontal as $x\\to \\pm \\infty .$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each equation.\n\n- The equation ${y}^{′}=\\frac{1}{1-{x}^{2}}$ does *not* give the required behavior. The right-hand side approaches $\\pm \\infty $ as $x\\to -1,$ but it also approaches $\\pm \\infty $ as $x\\to 1,$ which is not a behavior exhibited by our slope field.\n\n- The equation ${y}^{′}=-\\frac{8}{x+1}$ gives the required behavior. Notice that\n\n$$\n-\\frac{8}{x+1}\\to 0\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to \\pm \\infty .\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slopes of the slope field for this differential equation become horizontal as $x\\to \\pm \\infty .$\n\n- The equation ${y}^{′}=-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{x+1}$ does *not* give the required behavior. Notice that\n\n$$\n-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{x+1}=-\\frac{x}{(1+\\frac{1}{x})}\\to ∓\\infty \\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to \\pm \\infty .\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slopes of the slope field for this differential equation do not become horizontal as $x\\to \\pm \\infty .$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-115751/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "32b9a92b-880b-50de-b8e9-648fa219b83f"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-115751"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-115853/selection/d04369103e456c73e19787f8ce3463d11a0ad89b25762ce6dbfa0412fd462ad2" :answer/id #uuid "5853df92-ba4d-5cc4-bb29-007c2bb556e7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{y}^{′}=\\frac{1}{(x-1)^{2}}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-115853/selection/ca81eb51a2ae169ecd794feec4677718ae55620812ad4dda4f2ba1779d023170" :answer/id #uuid "64c490e6-f11c-5038-b740-a6fb059e230d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{y}^{′}=\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+x-2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-115853/selection/3dfdc1daf05b00430d5070ac9741dc7e22713f43dd5470dd9d62730ce3c71ed9" :answer/id #uuid "c2cb4fca-c312-5576-b40d-8fa135e643d2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{y}^{′}=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{x+2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-115853/selection/52af270915311f72c27db729f45cac79f9c29bbcd844c7632feaf5bf56c60df1" :answer/id #uuid "fa2f1ebf-6891-51b0-aa0b-a3c21aedff2a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{y}^{′}=\\frac{x+2}{1-{x}^{2}}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-115853/selection/f7531ef17a62b972f3188c82ee181f22bd9ad8e9bad1189308e0ddf00617db07" :answer/id #uuid "46a367b7-d1a4-5457-a05f-cad7d94548be" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{y}^{′}=\\frac{1}{2-x-{x}^{2}}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-115853/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "879098bc-c59a-5faa-a7c6-6b621608f1d0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-115853/selection/f7531ef17a62b972f3188c82ee181f22bd9ad8e9bad1189308e0ddf00617db07" :answer-field/choices ["q-115853/selection/d04369103e456c73e19787f8ce3463d11a0ad89b25762ce6dbfa0412fd462ad2" "q-115853/selection/ca81eb51a2ae169ecd794feec4677718ae55620812ad4dda4f2ba1779d023170" "q-115853/selection/3dfdc1daf05b00430d5070ac9741dc7e22713f43dd5470dd9d62730ce3c71ed9" "q-115853/selection/52af270915311f72c27db729f45cac79f9c29bbcd844c7632feaf5bf56c60df1" "q-115853/selection/f7531ef17a62b972f3188c82ee181f22bd9ad8e9bad1189308e0ddf00617db07"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-115853" :question/id #uuid "83a90d4b-eea7-5c39-bfee-4621ae6098ab" :question/math-academy-id "q-115853" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044310/assets/a355d9b35a7ebf791a56116b0ee5b127d0dfd90d6958b5bebd04aa9ad09a9ddc.png)\n\nIdentify the differential equation that generated the slope field above." :question/worked-solution "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044310/assets/6293d40f222d4d81236808f7ab0a35dfa82e1187b3a4d24bbc841a1b158f1039.png)\n\n \n\nNotice the following properties of the given slope field:\n\n- First, the solution curves have vertical asymptotes $x=-2$ and $x=1.$\n\nOf the given equations, the only two that satisfy this condition are\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\frac{1}{2-x-{x}^{2}}=-\\frac{1}{(x-1)(x+2)}\n$$\n\n \nand\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+x-2}=\\frac{x}{(x-1)(x+2)}.\n$$\n\n- Second, the lines $x=-2$ and $x=1$ divide the plane into $3$ regions. The sign of the slopes in each of the $3$ regions are easily deduced by considering the behavior of the solution curves in these regions. This information is summarized in the following table:\n\n| Region | $x<-2$ | $-2<x<1$ | $x>1$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Slope | $↘$ | $↗$ | $↘$ |\n| Sign | $-$ | $+$ | $-$ |\n\nOf the two remaining options, the only one that gives this behavior is\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\frac{1}{2-x-{x}^{2}}.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-115853/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "32b9a92b-880b-50de-b8e9-648fa219b83f"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-115853"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-96098"] :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044310/assets/680bc0001fb95783ee70fc88a7106997bcb3dba2ad85a708164878d0f610ecfb.png)\n\nWhich of the following equations could have generated the slope field shown above?\n\n- ${y}^{′}=x(x-2)$\n- ${y}^{′}=\\frac{5x}{x-2}$\n- ${y}^{′}=\\frac{1}{x-2}$" :question/worked-solution "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044310/assets/74b482b5b72421c9ee208034dc4727605e42874015d080130ffd7d18437af0da.png)\n\n \n\nNotice the following properties of the given slope field:\n\n- The solution curves have the vertical asymptote $x=2.$\n\n- The slopes of the slope field become horizontal as $x\\to \\pm \\infty .$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each equation.\n\n- The equation ${y}^{′}=x(x-2)$ does *not* give the required behavior. The right-hand side does not approach $\\pm \\infty $ as $x\\to 2.$\n\n- The equation ${y}^{′}=\\frac{5x}{x-2}$ does *not* give the required behavior. Notice that\n\n$$\n\\frac{5x}{x-2}=\\frac{5}{(1-\\frac{2}{x})}\\to 5\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to \\pm \\infty .\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slopes of the slope field for this differential equation do not become horizontal as $x\\to \\pm \\infty .$\n\n- The equation ${y}^{′}=\\frac{1}{x-2}$ gives the required behavior. Notice that\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{x-2}\\to 0\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to \\pm \\infty .\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slopes of the slope field for this differential equation become horizontal as $x\\to \\pm \\infty .$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "q-115852/selection/2688dfbbc99b8c5fa637932ddb809a1cfce3ce686c702af040e05308992535a0" :answer/id #uuid "3067ad22-375a-54b0-9c3c-075fa805b7c8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{y}^{′}=\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}-x-2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-115852/selection/5845c54f45ad7e9e87ce66538c2e755859d61535f85b63439fc9cfe7bd4b1e99" :answer/id #uuid "23fd4c7c-0a0b-5c35-9c75-a34232758c82" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{y}^{′}=\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}-1}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-115852/selection/c49aadcbc22cd8f37154e3232117bf3dbd1bac832b9645e5847f5b33a8b85a2c" :answer/id #uuid "e16e4487-2323-5f42-92bc-bb55c718ed55" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{y}^{′}=\\frac{1}{x-2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-115852/selection/1837f76445b36f6ae729950c801738c77dee54f6e18c8d36eabd9c74635da64a" :answer/id #uuid "d39ed27f-f059-5339-96a4-1f552c1bc2bf" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{y}^{′}=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}-x-2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-115852/selection/a5977eda37204dff63f989822ed071f84e117f6678825db7cd97009feafec218" :answer/id #uuid "58bd2e7f-03fa-5f9b-9c84-fce215644331" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{y}^{′}=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{x-2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-115852/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "8edbf045-8881-5ee6-964a-2bc6a14571c5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-115852/selection/2688dfbbc99b8c5fa637932ddb809a1cfce3ce686c702af040e05308992535a0" :answer-field/choices ["q-115852/selection/2688dfbbc99b8c5fa637932ddb809a1cfce3ce686c702af040e05308992535a0" "q-115852/selection/5845c54f45ad7e9e87ce66538c2e755859d61535f85b63439fc9cfe7bd4b1e99" "q-115852/selection/c49aadcbc22cd8f37154e3232117bf3dbd1bac832b9645e5847f5b33a8b85a2c" "q-115852/selection/1837f76445b36f6ae729950c801738c77dee54f6e18c8d36eabd9c74635da64a" "q-115852/selection/a5977eda37204dff63f989822ed071f84e117f6678825db7cd97009feafec218"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-115852" :question/id #uuid "01d0f4b2-f8b0-5360-b72e-8c82d7b84058" :question/math-academy-id "q-115852" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044310/assets/3e065edec23179a946ea28aa6f2b4f9a439ba23c014c38dded0cc7c756867a90.png)\n\nIdentify the differential equation that generated the slope field above." :question/worked-solution "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044310/assets/5b92aa0ecaf7dd906a39c44e31a12e725802d9c7c5cdc046351030ae04f6809e.png)\n\n \n\nNotice the following properties of the given slope field:\n\n- First, the solution curves have vertical asymptotes $x=-1$ and $x=2.$\n\nOf the given equations, the only two that satisfy this condition are\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}-x-2}=\\frac{1}{(x+1)(x-2)}\n$$\n\n \nand\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}-x-2}=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{(x+1)(x-2)}.\n$$\n\n- Second, the slopes of the slope field become horizontal as $x\\to \\pm \\infty .$ \n\nOf the two remaining options, the only one that gives this behavior is\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}-x-2}.\n$$\n\nNotice that\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}-x-2}\\to 0\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to \\pm \\infty .\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slopes of the slope field for the differential equation ${y}^{′}=\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}-x-2}$ become horizontal as $x\\to \\pm \\infty .$\n\nOn the other hand,\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}-x-2}=\\frac{1}{(1-\\frac{1}{x}-\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}})}\\to 1\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to \\pm \\infty .\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slopes of the slope field for the differential equation ${y}^{′}=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}-x-2}$ do *not* become horizontal as $x\\to \\pm \\infty .$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-115852/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "32b9a92b-880b-50de-b8e9-648fa219b83f"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-115852"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-8483"] :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044310/assets/df8fd1746a9043aa52213a7ae5fd57b7f6c7438d02f5993c99bc8bd294309f07.png)\n\n \n\nWhich of the following equations could have generated the slope field shown above?\n\n- $y' = (x+1)(x-3)$\n\n- $y'= (x+1)(x-2)(x-3)$\n\n- $y' = x+1$" :question/worked-solution "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044310/assets/180dafc23275d08fea5565296f3d72c92aee7ac2b4b68077b2c084d005a23456.png)\n\n \n\nNotice that the slopes of the slope field are horizontal along the lines $x = -1$ and $x = 3,$ as shown above. Therefore, the right-hand side of the differential equation should evaluate to zero on both of these lines.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each equation:\n\n- The equation $y'= (x+1)(x-3)$ gives the required behavior. The right-hand side evaluates to zero at $x=-1$ and $x=3.$\n\n- The equation $y'=(x+1)(x-2)(x-3)$ does *not* give the required behavior. The right-hand side evaluates to zero at $x=-1$ and $x=3,$ but it also evaluates to zero when $x=2,$ which is not a behavior exhibited by our slope field.\n\n- The equation $y' = x+1$ does *not* give the required behavior. The right-hand side does not evaluate to zero at both $x=-1$ and $x=3.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-8484"] :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044310/assets/3ba95acb3d7d009a482f79e1224c0628ff43b2aa149df0918768c74576cc4d9a.png)\n\n \n\nThe diagram shown above is the slope field for which of the following differential equations?\n\n- $y' = -x(x+4)^2$\n\n- $y' = -x^2(x+2)$\n\n- $y' = x^2(x+4)$" :question/worked-solution "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044310/assets/3fb7bf013703760852c86c2b16147b278fd1707931e980dbb693412832cdc5f1.png)\n\n \n\nNotice the following properties of the given slope field:\n\n- First, the slopes of the slope field are horizontal along the lines $x = -4$ and $x = 0.$ Therefore, the right-hand side of the differential equation should equal zero on both of these lines.\n\nOf the given equations, the only equations that satisfy this condition are the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =-x(x+4)^{2} \\\\ {y}^{′} & ={x}^{2}(x+4)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Second, the lines $x=-4$ and $x=0$ divide the plane into $3$ regions. The sign of the slopes in each of the $3$ regions is easily deduced by considering the behavior of the solution curves in these regions. This information is summarized in the following table:\n\n| Region | $x <  -4$ | $-4 <  x <  0$ | $x > 0$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Slope | $\\nearrow$ | $\\nearrow$ | $\\searrow$ |\n| Sign | $\\color{blue}+$ | $\\color{blue}+$ | $\\color{red}-$ |\n\nOf the two remaining options, the only one that gives this behavior is\n\n$$\ny' = -x(x+4)^2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $y'= -x(x+4)^2.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-9312"] :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044310/assets/b2b67d39de238bceb50e0f21d65d99434b4a7f3be553a6c1c12876ecef3e5cf1.png)\n\n \n\nWhich of the following equations could have generated the slope field shown above?\n\n- $y' =x^2 - 4$\n\n- $y' = x^2 - 4x + 4$\n\n- $y' = 4 - x^2$" :question/worked-solution "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044310/assets/b3768fbcd892e8484f0fabfca29bd6365cfc382bde2dd39c6ca8d278911747db.png)\n\n \n\nNotice the following properties of the given slope field:\n\n- First, the slope field is zero along the lines $x = -2$ and $x = 2.$ Therefore, the right-hand side of the differential equation should evaluate to zero on both of these lines.\n\n- Second, the lines $x=-2$ and $x=2$ divide the plane into $3$ regions. The sign of the slopes in each of the $3$ regions is easily deduced by considering the behavior of the solution curves in these regions. This information is summarized in the following table:\n\n| Region | $x <                  -2$ | $-2 <                  x <                  2$ | $x > 2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Slope | $\\nearrow$ | $\\searrow$ | $\\nearrow$ |\n| Slope | $\\color{blue}+$ | $\\color{red}-$ | $\\color{blue}+$ |\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each equation.\n\n- The equation \n\n$$\ny' =x^2 - 4 = (x+2)(x-2)\n$$\n\ngives the required behavior.\n\n- The equation\n\n$$\ny' = x^2 - 4x + 4 = (x-2)^2\n$$\n\ndoes *not* give the required behavior. The right-hand side evaluates to zero at $x=2,$ but not at $x=-2.$\n\n- The equation \n\n$$\ny' = 4 - x^2 = (2+x)(2-x)\n$$\n\ndoes *not* give the required behavior. The right-hand side evaluates to zero at $x=-2$ and $x=2,$ but the sign of the slopes in the required regions (summarized below) does not correspond to the given slope field.\n\n| Region | $x <                  -2$ | $-2 <                  x <                  2$ | $x > 2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Slope | $\\searrow$ | $\\nearrow$ | $\\searrow$ |\n| Sign | $\\color{red}-$ | $\\color{blue}+$ | $\\color{red}-$ |\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-8486"] :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044310/assets/49c8ba9f215b8ec05263c46eda9809728e918e94808023969c6b57d034470115.png)\n\n \n\nWhich of the following equations could have generated the slope field shown above?\n\n- $y' = \\dfrac{2}{(x+2)^2}$\n\n- $y' = \\dfrac{1}{x+2}$\n\n- $y' = \\dfrac{2x}{x+2}$" :question/worked-solution "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044310/assets/65bedbf95e886a45884a914cab0100bacca6dc849606b15fe3a74ba7a4dd7dda.png)\n\n \n\nNotice the following properties of the given slope field:\n\n- The solution curves have the vertical asymptote $x=-2.$\n\n- The slopes of the slope field become horizontal as $x\\to\\pm\\infty.$\n\n- The slopes of the slope field are positive for $x>-2$ and negative for $x< -2.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each equation.\n\n- The equation $y' =  \\dfrac{2}{(x+2)^2}$ does *not* give the required behavior. The slopes of the slope field for this differential equation are positive for all $x \\neq  -2.$\n\n- The equation $y' = \\dfrac{1}{x+2}$ gives the required behavior. Notice that the right-hand side of this equation is positive for $x>-2$ and negative for $x< -2,$ and $y'\\to 0$ as $x\\to\\pm\\infty.$\n\n- The equation $y' = \\dfrac{2x}{x+2}$ does *not* give the required behavior. Notice that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{2x}{x-2} = \\dfrac{2}{\\left(1-\\dfrac2x\\right)}\\to 2 \\quad\\text{as} \\quad x\\to \\pm\\infty.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slopes of the slope field for this differential equation do not become horizontal as $x\\to\\pm\\infty.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" :db/ensure [:question/validate]}]
